The documentation details the additional capabilities of the ApplicationContext that go beyond simple dependency injection and object lifecycle management.
While the low-level BeanFactory interface only handles holding and wiring beans, ApplicationContext extends several highly specialized interfaces to act as a complete application ecosystem engine.

1. Internationalization (i18n) via MessageSource

The ApplicationContext implements the MessageSource interface, allowing you to seamlessly resolve text strings into different human languages based on a user's location (Locale).
• How it works: Instead of hardcoding text like "Welcome to our store", you reference a property key like welcome.message.
• The Blueprint: Spring automatically searches for a registered bean named messageSource.
java
@Autowired
private ApplicationContext context;

public void printWelcome(Locale locale) {
    // Dynamically fetches the right language text file entry
    String msg = context.getMessage("welcome.message", null, "Default Welcome", locale);
    System.out.println(msg);
}
Use code with caution.

2. Event Publishing via ApplicationEventPublisher

The context acts as an in-memory event bus. Beans can communicate with each other asynchronously or loosely coupled by publishing and listening for custom state alerts without knowing who the recipient is.
• Publishing an Event: You pass any object or a subclass of ApplicationEvent into the context's publishEvent() method.
• Listening for an Event: Any bean can use the @EventListener annotation over a method to instantly catch and process that event when it fires.
java
@Component
public class OrderService {
    @Autowired
    private ApplicationContext context;

    public void completeOrder() {
        System.out.println("Order completed!");
        // Notify the rest of the application
        context.publishEvent(new OrderCompletedEvent(this, "Order #1234"));
    }
}
Use code with caution.

3. Resource Loading via ResourcePatternResolver

The ApplicationContext functions as a unified utility for reading file resources (such as text files, XML configs, or image metadata) from various internal or external system structures.
• The Prefix Magic: It understands specific protocol prefixes to figure out where to find your files:
	• classpath:config/settings.properties (Searches inside your build JAR file structure)
	• file:///var/data/logs.txt (Searches your operating system's local storage disk)
	• https://example.com (Fetches the resource live over the web)
java
Resource resource = context.getResource("classpath:banner.txt");
// Read stream cleanly from the resolved abstract location
Use code with caution.

Summary Checklist: BeanFactory vs. ApplicationContext


Capability	BeanFactory	ApplicationContext
Core Dependency Injection / Wiring	Yes	Yes
Internationalization (MessageSource)	No	Yes
Internal Event Bus Network	No	Yes
Uniform Cloud/Local File Loading	No	Yes
Automatic BeanPostProcessor Registration	No (Must register manually)	Yes (Discovers them automatically)


