The documentation explains that the ApplicationContext acts as a highly specialized ResourceLoader. This gives your beans a convenient, unified way to read low-level file resources (like properties, text schemas, JSON metadata, or image streams) from completely different physical locations without you needing to write custom file-handling code.
Instead of using raw Java file inputs—which force you to write different code depending on whether a file is on your hard drive, inside your JAR, or on the internet—Spring wraps them all in a single abstract interface called Resource.

1. The Strategy Prefix Magic 🔮

The ApplicationContext determines how and where to fetch your low-level file asset by inspecting a location path prefix. You pass a simple string, and Spring handles the rest:
• classpath:data/config.json: Searches inside your compiled JAR file's resources folder. (This is the most common approach for immutable project assets).
• file:///var/exports/report.csv: Bypasses the application package entirely and pulls the file straight off the operating system's local hard drive storage.
• https://mycompany.com: Dynamically downloads the low-level file asset directly over the web at runtime.

2. How to Use It Cleanly in Code


Option A: Direct Parameter Injection (Recommended)

You don't even need to touch the ApplicationContext directly. You can use the @Value annotation to tell Spring to resolve the file path and hand you a ready-to-read Resource object automatically:
java
@Component
public class ReportAnalyzer {

    // Spring locates the file inside the JAR, opens it, and injects it here
    @Value("classpath:templates/report-template.txt")
    private Resource templateResource;

    public void readTemplate() throws IOException {
        // Read text content natively using standard stream tooling
        InputStream stream = templateResource.getInputStream();
        // ... process data stream cleanly
    }
}
Use code with caution.

Option B: Implementing ResourceLoaderAware

If your application dynamically determines which file path to read based on incoming user request logic, you can implement the ResourceLoaderAware interface. Spring will inject a low-level reference to the context's resource loader strategy at startup:
java
@Component
public class DynamicFileManager implements ResourceLoaderAware {

    private ResourceLoader resourceLoader;

    @Override
    public void setResourceLoader(ResourceLoader resourceLoader) {
        this.resourceLoader = resourceLoader; // Spring automatically passes this in
    }

    public void processFile(String customPath) {
        // customPath could dynamically be a file:// url or a classpath: url
        Resource target = resourceLoader.getResource(customPath);
        System.out.println("File loaded status: " + target.exists());
    }
}
Use code with caution.

